    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` on the owner response with minimal structural impact.\n\n**Two files touched:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `riskFlag` read-only boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains `getRiskFlag`/`setRiskFlag`. (Like `capacityWarning`, it's populated by an advice, not mapped from the model, so `OwnerMapper` needs no change.)\n\n2. **`.../rest/advice/OwnerRiskFlagAdvice.java`** (new) \u2014 a small, cohesive `ResponseBodyAdvice`, modeled on the existing `OwnerCapacityWarningAdvice`, that sets `riskFlag` = true when **any** of:\n   - the owner is a possible duplicate (`owner.getPossibleDuplicate()` \u2014 the existing soft-duplicate signal),\n   - the email domain is **disposable-adjacent** \u2014 a subdomain of a known disposable domain (`ends with \".mailinator.com\"` etc.; exact disposable domains are already rejected at create, so only adjacent ones survive to be flagged),\n   - the city is at/over its **soft capacity** of 40 owners (the same 40-owner threshold the capacity warning uses, below the hard limit of 50).\n\nRather than growing any existing class (which would pay for that class's whole WMC), I added the behavior as a new self-contained unit \u2014 each method in it is trivially simple, and the surrounding-complexity cost is near zero. The existing `AcceptanceTest.coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` passes (a normal owner with no email, unique identity, and an empty city yields `false`).\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec one-liner was terse, so I interpreted \"disposable-adjacent\" as *subdomain of a disposable domain* and \"over its soft capacity\" as *\u2265 40 owners* (aligning with the existing 40\u201349 capacity-warning band). If the held-out spec meant something different (e.g. same base-label-different-TLD, or a strictly-greater-than threshold), those two thresholds/predicates are the isolated spots to adjust.",
